 "case:accusation.successTitle": "The last track, traced",
 "case:briefing.0": "Rookery Audio occupies a converted warehouse on Brighton’s North Quay: two recording studios, a small archive, and a late-night radio stream that made its founder famous. On Friday 16 October, the company is hours away from announcing its sale to Vesper Media for eighteen million pounds.",
 "case:briefing.1": "At 22:30, Marcus Vale, 51, Rookery’s founder and CEO, was found dead in Studio B. He had been struck once with a heavy object from the studio. The door was ajar. Nothing obvious was stolen, and the building’s front door was still locked from the inside.",
 "case:briefing.2": "The police believe Marcus died between 22:10 and 22:25. At 23:00, his company’s flagship late-night programme began exactly on time, with host Tom Hargreaves sounding relaxed on air. Tom says he had been live in Studio A all evening.",
 "case:briefing.3": "Marcus’s sister Clare has asked you to look before the police make an arrest. She says her brother was difficult, vain, and very good at hearing the one false note in a room. ‘He was about to sell the thing he built,’ she says. ‘If somebody killed him for it, I want to know what they thought they were saving.’",
 "case:briefing.4": "Everybody who worked late at Rookery is still in Brighton. The sale announcement is paused, the archive is sealed, and the station’s last broadcast is still in the system. Find out what happened before the company’s own story becomes the only one anyone remembers.",
 "case:epilogue.0": "By midnight, the police had taken over the Rookery building and the Vesper announcement was cancelled. The station’s stream played a silent holding loop until dawn, while the people who had spent years filling the archive boxes waited in the corridor outside.",
 "case:epilogue.1": "Clare Vale refused the first offer from Vesper Media. The sale eventually went through the following spring, but the archive stayed in Brighton, under a trust that paid its royalties to the people who had made the programmes. Tom’s late-night show returned with a new producer and a strict rule: no one was allowed to call a recording live.",
 "case:epilogue.2": "Ben kept the old automation rack in a glass case. He labelled the button that had fooled everyone for one night: AUTO-RESCUE. Underneath, in Marcus’s handwriting, someone added a second label. LISTEN CLOSELY.",
 "case:epilogue.3": "Rookery’s archive still has a gap on one shelf where the Clearwater file used to be. The photocopy is kept in a locked drawer now, beside the master keys and the brass reel weight. Nobody at the company calls the empty space a memorial. They call it a warning.",
